WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.010
Norm Peterson was one of the regular patrons
on Cheers, but where did George Wendt, the

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.520
actor who played him, go after the bar closed
up?

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.340
We've got the details right here.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.160
While Cheers was still on the air, Wendt began
appearing in a series of sketches on Saturday

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.060
Night Live as Bob Swerski, a diehard Chicago
sports fan and host of the show "Bill Swerski's

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:19.820
Superfans."

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:20.820
"Da Bears!"

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:22.660
"Da Bears!"

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.000
Wendt is a Chicago native himself, and as
a lifelong supporter of all Windy City sports

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.320
teams, he was instantly relatable to any other
local fans who were watching.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.180
The sketches originally aired in the early
nineties, shortly before Cheers ended in 1993.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.720
But that wasn't the end of Bob Swerski or
his passionate commentary on Chicago sports.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.790
Wendt has returned to SNL to play Swerski
several times, with a total of nine appearances

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.020
between 1991 and 2003.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.660
He's also shown up in character as Bob outside
of SNL.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:55.180
In 2019, for example, he stopped by the ESPN+
series Peyton's Places, in which Peyton Manning

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.020
meets with a variety of interesting figures
to explore the history of football and the

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.580
cultural impact of the sport.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:05.980
"Baby Peytonmy son, are you ready to be baptized
into the Bears superfandom?"

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.880
"Well, I'm not drunk, so no.”

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.100
A couple years after Cheers ended, Wendt got
his own starring sitcom gig.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.280
Alas, The George Wendt Show reached nowhere
near the success of Cheers, airing for only

00:01:18.289 --> 00:01:20.810
one season on CBS in 1995.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:24.780
Perhaps a sitcom actually based around Norm
Peterson, whom the audience already knew and

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:25.960
loved, would've been a better move.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.960
It took a few years after The George Wendt
Show wrapped for Wendt to nab another regular

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:30.580
TV gig.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.900
In 1997, he appeared on the sitcom The Naked
Truth for three episodes.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:39.100
Then in 2001, he appeared on another comedy
show, but this time, it was a series with

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.479
a different kind of audience than he was used
to, as he checked in for six episodes of Sabrina,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.400
the Teenage Witch.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.160
And in 2006, he was on the one-season WB sitcom
Modern Men as a guy who dished out dating

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.140
advice to confused, single men, even though
he'd been through a few divorces himself.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.100
None of the shows that Wendt worked on after
leaving Boston really got the same reception

00:01:58.109 --> 00:02:01.679
that Cheers did, but of course, that's the
type of iconic show that doesn't come around

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.240
very often.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:03.120
And Wendt knows it.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.940
While reflecting on the show's enduring popularity,
he told the Toronto Sun in 2017,

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.040
"It's such a pleasant surprise that something
you were a part of could make such an impact."

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.080
Besides Cheers, Wendt's longest-running TV
gig in terms of episode count was on the TBS

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.080
sitcom Clipped.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.940
He played Buzzy, a guy who used to own a barbershop
named, naturally enough, Buzzy's.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.310
In the show, he still works there, but now
the place is owned and managed by a fellow

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.020
named Ben.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.540
Back in the day, Ben was an unpopular kid
in high school, but now that he's the boss

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.400
at Buzzy's, he's in charge of a few of his
old classmates.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.820
Naturally, jealousy and petty antics ensue.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.060
As for Buzzy, he might be beyond all that
high school drama, but he's got relationship

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.620
problems of his own, like figuring out how
to propose to the man of his dreams.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:48.140
The show ran for ten episodes in the summer
of 2015 and was officially cancelled a few

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.790
months after the last episode aired.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.340
On Cheers, George Wendt starred alongside
some pretty impressive names in the world

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:54.840
of comedy.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.320
But recently, he's started working with someone
even more important to him: his wife, Bernadette

00:02:59.320 --> 00:02:59.820
Birkett.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.440
The couple starred together in a 2013 production
of the comedy play Never Too Late.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.960
The original version of this show was popular
on Broadway in the 1960s, but it's not a very

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.100
well-known play today.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.900
However, Wendt and Birkett's version was well-received
and positively reviewed.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.130
In the play, Wendt played Harry Lambert, a
successful and traditionally-minded man who

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:22.160
owns a lumber company and thinks that his
wife, Edith, truly enjoys waiting on him hand

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:22.660
and foot.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:27.300
But when Edith comes home with news that she's
pregnant, Harry is totally caught off guard.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.480
Wendt and Birkett were praised for bringing
their real-life chemistry to the stage.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.890
Robert Trussell of The Kansas City Star noted,

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:37.280
"The bombastic Harry is the showier role,
but Birkett goes for relatively subtle humor

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.120
in a precise, thoughtful performance.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.340
For his part, Wendt delivers a master class
on comedic timing."

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.120
Although most of Wendt's acting work has been
on TV, he’s had plenty of film credits to

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:47.920
his name as well.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.680
His career started with bit parts in movies,
and he appeared in a couple films that you

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.230
probably enjoyed as a kid.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:58.580
In 1994, for example, he had a memorable supporting
role as a lumberyard clerk in The Little Rascals.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.350
And of course we can't forget Spice World,
in which he played one of the guys pitching

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.220
film ideas for the girls to star in.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.780
Wendt's more recent film credits include the
2017 Adam Sandler Netflix vehicle Sandy Wexler

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.020
and the comedy The Climb, which screened at
the 2019 Cannes Film Festival.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.660
He's also got multiple projects in post-production.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:20.380
And maybe one day we'll see the return of
Norm and the rest of the gang on the big screen.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.639
When The Toronto Star asked him in 2017 about
the possibility of a Cheers reboot, he said,

00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:29.430
"I think some sort of reboot is inevitable,
whether it's a movie or a show."

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.449
Alas, he also added,

00:04:30.449 --> 00:04:31.640
"But I wouldn't hold my breath."

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.480
"Well, hear hear, huh?"

